[Johnny Castaway now runs in my browser tab](https://khawkins98.github.io/johnny_web/). I first met him in 1993 or '94, during a summer break visit to my dad's office. On a Windows 3.1 PC, a little man fished, slept, built a raft and got into trouble on an island whenever nobody was using the computer. I kept watching to see what he'd do next.

I found him again decades later, along with the work others had done to preserve and revive the screensaver. An emulator could run the original, but opening one for a few minutes with Johnny missed the casual pleasure of seeing him appear in the corner of a screen.

So I built on an earlier JavaScript port. I wanted to leave the island running in a tab, and to understand what made it tick: which scenes were scripted, how the story advanced, and how I could check the remake against the original program.

> **tl;dr**
>
> - Johnny's island combines scripted gags and weighted random choices with an eleven-day story that advances at most one step per calendar day.
> - The browser port builds on earlier reverse-engineering work and reads the original game resources.
> - Running the original Windows program under instrumentation provides reference traces; separate rendering “goldens” catch changes in the port.

If you have never seen Johnny in action, [Nostalgia Nerd's look at the screensaver](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=E5lxiTJGqHw) is a good place to start. You can see what the fuss is about without setting up my browser version.

