  <figcaption class="kh-figure__caption">A quieter moment: Johnny asleep under the moon, captured in Enhanced mode.</figcaption>
  <small class="kh-figure__credit">Screenshot from johnny_web. Original artwork by Dynamix / Sierra On-Line.</small>
</figure>

Jeff Tunnell, who helped create Johnny Castaway, [described its basic rhythm](https://jefftunnell.com/johnny-castaway-common-questions/):

> “So it generally runs a little loop that is interrupted by random events.”

The variety has two layers. Within a day, the program chooses among eligible scenes using weighted randomness. Across days, it follows an [eleven-day story](https://github.com/khawkins98/johnny_web/blob/main/docs/story-over-time.md), with particular events tied to particular days.

Playing a day's key scene unlocks the next one. When the calendar date changes, the saved day moves one step towards that unlocked day. It doesn't skip ahead to catch up with every day you've been away, and day eleven wraps back to day one. The browser port keeps this progress in local storage.

The tide follows a separate half-hour clock; holiday decorations follow calendar dates. The raft's progress, the island's position, and whether a sequence plays by day or night also belong to the program's scene planning.

That longer rhythm is part of what I wanted to bring back. You can watch a gag, leave, and return to a world with a little continuity. It is a story designed to be encountered in passing.

## Inside a gag, and between gags

The original files contain artwork, sound, and small programs describing the animations. They use the **Dynamix Game Development System**, or DGDS. The browser engine, which I've called Bottle DGDS, decodes those resources and runs their instructions in JavaScript.

There are two useful levels to understand:

- **ADS scripts coordinate a gag.** They start and stop animations, make choices, and wait for other actions to finish.
- **TTM scripts perform the animation.** They load images, draw sprite frames, wait, play sounds, and loop or finish.

The [“Eight Arm Menace” scene flow](https://github.com/khawkins98/johnny_web/blob/main/docs/scene-flows/FISHING.ADS.md#eight-arm-menace-fishingads-tag-3) makes that concrete: Johnny gets his pole, casts it, reels in a heavy catch, and reaches the octopus gag. Several animations can be active together. Their order and timing determine whether the result reads as one coherent joke.

Choosing which gag comes next is another job. The original Windows executable had a director around those scripts. It chose an eligible final scene, filled in compatible everyday scenes before it, and arranged Johnny's walks between their starting and ending positions. The browser port reconstructs that [scene-selection behaviour](https://github.com/khawkins98/johnny_web/blob/main/docs/johnny-host-behavior.md) separately from the script interpreter.

That distinction exposed a gap in the earlier web host: it could decode and play scenes that ordinary playback never selected. The files were there, but the director needed reconstructing. Decoding another animation would not tell the program what to play next.

## Asking the original what it actually does

I started with [Alexandre Fontoura's Castaway](https://github.com/xesf/castaway), which already had a JavaScript foundation for decoding the resources. Jérémie Guillaume's [Johnny Reborn](https://github.com/jno6809/jc_reborn), Hans Milling's [C# remake](https://github.com/nivs1978/Johnny-Castaway-Open-Source), and the [ScummVM DGDS engine](https://github.com/scummvm/scummvm/tree/master/engines/dgds) provided further findings and behavioural references. This project owes a great deal to that work.

Decoding the files gives you instructions to interpret. Examining the executable helps recover the rules around them. An interpretation can still be wrong in ways that look plausible on screen.

The useful reference is the original program running. The project's [capture tools](https://github.com/khawkins98/johnny_web/blob/main/tools/faithfulness-oracle/METHODOLOGY.md) use Windows 3.1 inside a patched DOSBox-X. The patch lets the harness isolate a gag and record its animation threads over time. The captures use the same game-data version as the browser port, so differences aren't caused by comparing two releases.

