I compare the two versions in four steps:

1. Run the same gag in the original program several times, allowing for its random branches.
2. Turn those recordings into reference fingerprints: which animation threads are active together, their peak count, and their lifespans.
3. Run the browser engine and compare its timeline against those references.
4. Investigate differences, with visual checks where the traces don't settle the question.

One finding was particularly revealing. Treating random branches equally made a fishing sequence exit about three times too often. The original data assigns different weights. Both versions could display a plausible fishing trip and reach an ending; the frequency of the choices was wrong.

Timing has similar traps. How long an animation is allowed to run is separate from how long each frame should wait. Confusing those clocks changes how actions overlap, even when the individual drawings are correct.

There is a second kind of reference in the project: [rendering goldens](https://github.com/khawkins98/johnny_web/blob/main/test/render-goldens.mjs). These replay selected fragile sequences with repeatable inputs and save compact fingerprints of the drawing operations and resulting pixels. A later code change that alters the output makes the check fail, so the difference can be reviewed.

The original-program recordings help establish what the port should do. The goldens preserve reviewed behaviour in the port, including any mistake that made it into the baseline. Neither check proves that every pixel, sound, or random path is correct. The [coverage report](https://github.com/khawkins98/johnny_web/blob/main/docs/oracle-coverage.md) records the comparisons and their gaps.

I used AI heavily in the implementation, reverse-engineering, and testing. The running original gave that work something concrete to answer to. A convincing explanation of an instruction became a hypothesis to check against a trace, rather than a result to accept on its own.

## Back on the island

The [browser version](https://khawkins98.github.io/johnny_web/) offers Classic and Enhanced playback. Classic keeps the native scale, static clouds and original waves; Enhanced adds responsive scaling and moving clouds and waves. The original Dynamix and Sierra artwork, sound, and gags are still the reason to watch.

You need to supply the original data. The [README](https://github.com/khawkins98/johnny_web#readme) links the supported floppy archive; drop its ZIP or extracted `.ima` into the page and the app imports the resources into browser storage.

Like my [recent Mac OS 9 work](/posts/20260810-newsheep-sheepshaver-mac-os-9-2-apple-silicon-jit/), this started with wanting to spend time with old software again. Johnny's small island turned out to have enough machinery to keep me occupied long after I had him running.

I'd love to hear which gag you remember, or where you first saw him. I still like having that little man getting on with his day in the corner of a screen.

